WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:04.400
Now the methods that we're going to use in order to load the data from the source to the data warehouse

00:04.440 --> 00:06.080
is the bulk inserts.

00:06.120 --> 00:13.280
Bulk insert is a method of loading massive amounts of data very quickly from files like CSV files,

00:13.280 --> 00:16.840
or maybe a text file directly into a database.

00:16.880 --> 00:22.400
It is not like the classical normal inserts where it can go and insert the data row by row, but instead

00:22.400 --> 00:28.240
the bulk insert is one operation that's going to load all the data in one go into the database, and

00:28.240 --> 00:29.960
that's what makes it very fast.

00:30.000 --> 00:31.600
So let's go and use this method.

00:31.640 --> 00:31.800
Okay.

00:31.840 --> 00:37.120
So now let's start writing the script in order to load the first table in the source CRM.

00:37.120 --> 00:42.760
So we're going to go and load the table customer info from the CSV file to the database table.

00:42.760 --> 00:44.160
So the syntax is very simple.

00:44.160 --> 00:46.920
We're going to start with saying bulk insert.

00:46.920 --> 00:49.760
So with that SQL understand we are doing not a normal insert.

00:49.760 --> 00:51.240
We are doing a bulk insert.

00:51.240 --> 00:53.720
And then we have to go and specify the table name.

00:53.720 --> 00:58.680
So it is bronze dot CRM cost info.

00:58.680 --> 01:04.550
So now we have to specify the full location of the file that we are trying to load in this table.

01:04.590 --> 01:08.550
So now what we have to do is to go and get the path where the file is stored.

01:08.590 --> 01:14.230
So I'm going to go and copy the whole path and then add it to the bulk insert exactly like where the

01:14.230 --> 01:15.350
data exists.

01:15.350 --> 01:21.030
So for me it is in C SQL Data Warehouse project datasets in the source CRM.

01:21.350 --> 01:23.510
And then I have to specify the file name.

01:23.510 --> 01:27.070
So it's going to be the cost underscore info dot csv.

01:27.230 --> 01:31.910
You have to get it exactly like the path of your files, otherwise it will not be working.

01:31.910 --> 01:35.230
So after the path now we come to the With clause.

01:35.270 --> 01:38.870
Now we have to tell the SQL server how to handle our file.

01:38.870 --> 01:40.790
So here comes the specifications.

01:40.790 --> 01:43.070
There is a lot of stuff that we can define.

01:43.070 --> 01:46.390
So let's start with the very important one is the row header.

01:46.430 --> 01:53.030
Now if you check the content of our files you can see always the first row includes the header information

01:53.030 --> 01:54.030
of the file.

01:54.030 --> 01:56.590
So those informations are actually not the data.

01:56.590 --> 01:58.430
It's just the column names.

01:58.430 --> 02:04.510
The actual data starts from the second row, and we have to tell the database about this information.

02:04.510 --> 02:08.910
So we're gonna say first row is actually the second row.

02:08.910 --> 02:12.910
So with that we are telling SQL to skip the first row in the file.

02:12.910 --> 02:18.350
We don't need to load those informations because we have already defined the structure of our table.

02:18.350 --> 02:23.870
So this is the first specifications the next one which is as well very important in loading any CSV

02:23.910 --> 02:28.310
file is the separator between fields, the delimiter between fields.

02:28.310 --> 02:31.710
So here it's really depend on the file structure that you are getting from the source.

02:31.710 --> 02:35.670
As you can see, all those values are splitted with a comma.

02:35.670 --> 02:39.230
And we call this comma as a file separator or a delimiter.

02:39.230 --> 02:44.630
And I saw a lot of different csvs like sometimes they use a semicolon or a pipe or special character

02:44.630 --> 02:45.990
like a hash and so on.

02:45.990 --> 02:48.950
So you have to understand how the values are splitted.

02:48.950 --> 02:51.230
And in this file it's splitted by the comma.

02:51.230 --> 02:53.510
And we have to tell SQL about this info.

02:53.510 --> 02:54.270
It's very important.

02:54.270 --> 02:56.670
So we can say field terminator.

02:56.830 --> 02:59.300
And then we're going to say it is the comma.

02:59.460 --> 03:05.100
And basically those two informations are very important for SQL in order to be able to read your CSV

03:05.140 --> 03:05.540
file.

03:05.580 --> 03:11.020
Now there are like many different options that you can go and add for example tape lock.

03:11.060 --> 03:17.100
It is an option in order to improve the performance where you are locking the entire table during loading

03:17.100 --> 03:17.340
it.

03:17.380 --> 03:22.500
So as SQL is loading the data to this table, it's going to go and lock the whole table.

03:22.500 --> 03:25.140
So that's it for now I'm just going to go and add the semicolon.

03:25.140 --> 03:28.900
And let's go and insert the data from the file inside our bronze table.

03:28.900 --> 03:29.820
Let's execute it.

03:29.820 --> 03:34.220
And now we can see SQL did insert around 80,000 rows inside our table.

03:34.220 --> 03:35.060
So it is working.

03:35.060 --> 03:37.260
We just loaded the file into our database.

03:37.260 --> 03:40.020
But now it is not enough to just write the script.

03:40.020 --> 03:45.060
You have to test the quality of your bronze table, especially if you are working with files.

03:45.060 --> 03:47.500
So let's go and just do a simple select.

03:47.820 --> 03:49.260
So from our new table.

03:51.380 --> 03:52.980
And let's run it.

03:52.980 --> 03:57.170
So now the first thing that I check is do we have data like in each column.

03:57.170 --> 03:57.850
Well, yes.

03:57.850 --> 03:59.250
As you can see, we have data.

03:59.250 --> 04:03.210
And the second thing is do we have the data in the correct column.

04:03.210 --> 04:07.330
This is very critical as you are loading the data from a file to a database.

04:07.330 --> 04:09.330
Do we have the data in the correct column.

04:09.330 --> 04:12.290
So for example here we have the first name which of course makes sense.

04:12.290 --> 04:13.650
And here we have the last name.

04:13.650 --> 04:14.730
But what could happen.

04:14.730 --> 04:20.410
And this mistakes happens a lot is that you find the first name informations inside the key.

04:20.450 --> 04:25.610
And as well you see the last name inside the first name and the status inside the last name.

04:25.610 --> 04:28.010
So there is like shifting of the data.

04:28.010 --> 04:32.890
And this data engineering mistake is very common if you are working with CSV files and they are like

04:32.890 --> 04:38.490
different reasons why it happens, maybe the definition of your table is wrong or the field separator

04:38.490 --> 04:39.170
is wrong.

04:39.210 --> 04:41.370
Maybe it's not a comma, it's something else.

04:41.410 --> 04:47.130
Or the separator is a bad separator because sometimes maybe in the keys or in the first name, there

04:47.130 --> 04:50.770
is a comma and the SQL is not able to split the data correctly.

04:50.770 --> 04:53.970
So the quality of the CSV file is not really good.

04:54.010 --> 04:58.960
And there are many different reasons why you are not getting the data in the correct column, but for

04:58.960 --> 05:01.000
now, everything looks fine for us.

05:01.040 --> 05:05.400
And the next step that I go and count the rows inside this table.

05:05.520 --> 05:10.560
So let's go and select that so we can see we have 18,493.

05:10.600 --> 05:15.880
And now what we can do we can go to our CSV file and check how many rows do we have inside this file.

05:15.880 --> 05:19.440
And as you can see we have 18,494.

05:19.560 --> 05:20.600
We are almost there.

05:20.640 --> 05:23.120
There is like one extra row inside the file.

05:23.120 --> 05:25.040
And that's because of the header.

05:25.320 --> 05:28.800
The first header information is not loaded inside our table.

05:28.800 --> 05:33.960
And that's why always in our tables we are going to have one less row than the original files.

05:33.960 --> 05:35.200
So everything looks nice.

05:35.200 --> 05:36.880
And we have done this step correctly.

05:36.880 --> 05:39.960
Now if I go and run it again what can happen?

05:40.160 --> 05:43.160
We will get duplicates inside the bronze layer.

05:43.200 --> 05:48.840
So now we have loaded the file like twice inside the same table, which is naturally correct.

05:48.840 --> 05:55.240
The method that we have discussed is first to make the table empty and then load truncate, and then

05:55.280 --> 05:55.920
inserts.

05:55.920 --> 06:01.920
In order to do that before the bulk inserts, what we're going to do, we're going to say truncate table.

06:01.920 --> 06:06.760
And then we're going to have our table and that's it with a semicolon.

06:06.760 --> 06:13.680
So now what we are doing is first we are making the table empty and then we start loading from the scratch.

06:13.680 --> 06:17.040
We are loading the whole content of the file inside a table.

06:17.040 --> 06:18.680
And this is what we call full load.

06:18.680 --> 06:22.040
So now let's go and mark everything together and execute.

06:22.040 --> 06:28.000
And again if you go and check the content of the table you can see we have only 18,000 rows.

06:28.040 --> 06:29.760
Let's go and run it again.

06:29.800 --> 06:31.320
The count of the bronze layer.

06:31.320 --> 06:33.640
You can see we still have the 18,000.

06:33.640 --> 06:40.360
So each time you run the script now we are refreshing the table customer info from the file into the

06:40.360 --> 06:41.160
database table.

06:41.160 --> 06:43.600
So we are refreshing the bronze layer table.

06:43.600 --> 06:49.240
So that means if there is like now any changes in the file, it will be loaded to the table.

06:49.240 --> 06:55.070
So this is how you do a full load in the bronze layer by truncating the table and then doing the inserts.

06:55.110 --> 07:00.230
And now, of course, what we have to do is to pause the video and go and write the same script for

07:00.230 --> 07:01.510
all six files.

07:01.590 --> 07:02.910
So let's go and do that.

07:08.430 --> 07:08.790
Okay.

07:08.830 --> 07:09.150
Back.

07:09.150 --> 07:11.910
So I hope that you have as well written all those scripts.

07:11.910 --> 07:17.190
So I have the three tables in order to load the first source system and then three sections in order

07:17.190 --> 07:18.910
to load the second source system.

07:18.910 --> 07:22.270
And as I'm writing those scripts, make sure to have the correct path.

07:22.270 --> 07:26.270
So for the second source system you have to go and change the path for the other folder.

07:26.270 --> 07:30.990
And as well don't forget that table name on the bronze layer is different from the file name, because

07:30.990 --> 07:34.270
we start always with the source system name with the files.

07:34.270 --> 07:35.150
We don't have that.

07:35.150 --> 07:37.710
So now I think I have everything is ready.

07:37.710 --> 07:40.150
So let's go and execute the whole thing.

07:40.190 --> 07:40.670
Perfect.

07:40.670 --> 07:41.150
Awesome.

07:41.150 --> 07:42.990
So everything is working.

07:42.990 --> 07:48.750
Let me check the messages so we can see from the message how many rows are inserted in each table.

